    "q-333184": {
      "dom_id": "step-q333184",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=6x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?",
      "worked_solution": "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=4$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{6t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{6t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{6t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14121715/assets/2473117cdca4318187b94374820c0a12576e998e7f631a6e7bf51e56c985be43.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{6t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{4t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{4t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{6t})^{′}}=\\frac{4{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}}{6{c}_{1}{e}^{6t}}=\\frac{2{c}_{2}}{3{c}_{1}}{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as we move away from the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14121715/assets/e8110ea6d6a113e72e35502030afb495a23bc81106d64874582d03aae81b7c73.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
