    "q-333226": {
      "dom_id": "step-q333226",
      "name": "Question 20",
      "problem": "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below regarding the given decoupled system of linear differential equations.$\\,$\nThe $x$-axis is a {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-axis is a {{field-2}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-3}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis and also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a stable node (sink)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14121715/assets/1c4133695eeb868f563f80021755fea75bef622e1ad46cbbf6daff1c953cb075.png)",
